  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1239 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA negatively tilted trough is moving over the area this afternoon. \nMoisture has increased significantly at mid and upper levels as this \nsystem approached the area. As of early this afternoon, the DCVZ \nwas just to the east of Denver, with very light showers to its \neast and north. We still have a bit of a dry layer around 750mb \nacross the plains, and with how light the precip is, it could \nstruggle to make it to the ground in some areas until the layer \nstarts to saturate more. Overall rain amounts are expected to be \nvery light for much of the plains. Snow showers are moving into \nthe mountains from the west and south as of early this afternoon. \nCoverage of snow showers this afternoon and evening will be best \nsouth of I-70, with most likely accumulations around a trace to 3 \ninches above 9000ft. Elevations around 8000ft to 9000ft could see \na rain/snow mix today, but accumulations are unlikely. \n\nOvernight, we're expecting more fog development across the plains. \nThe REFS currently has a medium to high chance (50-75%) that \nportions of the I-70 and northern I-25 corridors will see fog with\nvisiblities less than 0.5 mile in the early morning. Fog should \nstart to clear around 10AM give or take an hour. \n\nUpper level ridging begins to build over the area again on \nSunday. Warmer and drier conditions will prevail Sunday through \nmuch of Tuesday. On Tuesday, an upper level trough will move onto\nthe West Coast. Winds at mid levels will strengthen as the \npressure gradient tightens between the upper ridge and trough. \nHowever, the strongest winds are forecast to remain to the south \nand east of our area. The trough will weaken and move east, \nsending a shortwave through the area early Wednesday. Precip \nchances will increase late Tuesday in the higher terrain as the \nshortwave approaches. The shortwave will be moving rather quickly \nand the southwesterly to westerly winds aloft are not super \nfavorable for our area, so impacts from this event will be rather \nlimited with snow totals around an inch or less for the higher \nelevations. For the plains, a cold front is forecast to move \nthrough sometime Wednesday, bringing slightly cooler temps for \nWednesday into the end of the work week. Rain chances remain low \n(<20%) for the plains with the this system.\n\nThat shortwave will quickly move off to the northeast as an upper \nlow moves onto the West Coast. The progression of this upper \nlow/trough Thursday into the weekend is highly uncertain. A \ncouple of days ago, models had a slower moving closed low headed \ntowards the four corners region, now the most recent runs have a \nquicker moving more open wave going over and out of the area by \nlate Saturday. The former is much more favorable for snowfall \nacross our area, which is why we've seen a steady downward trend \nin precip amounts in the ensemble members. However, we are still \nquite a ways out, so things could swing back the other direction.\nWe'll be keeping a close eye on this system.",
